1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,480
Section 3

2
00:00:01,480 --> 00:00:02,205
BETH:

3
00:00:02,205 --> 00:00:02,889
Oh good morning.

4
00:00:02,889 --> 00:00:04,861
You must be James.

5
00:00:04,861 --> 00:00:07,820
I'm Beth Cartwright - please call me Beth.

6
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:08,347
JAMES:

7
00:00:08,347 --> 00:00:09,400
Thank you.

8
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:09,768
BETH:

9
00:00:09,768 --> 00:00:14,917
Now as this is your first tutorial since you started on the Scandinavian Studies

10
00:00:14,917 --> 00:00:18,228
course, I'd like to find out something about you.

11
00:00:18,228 --> 00:00:21,170
Why did you decide to take this course?

12
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:21,530
JAMES:

13
00:00:21,530 --> 00:00:23,330
Well, my mother is Danish,

14
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:28,370
and although we always lived in England, she used to talk about her home

15
00:00:28,370 --> 00:00:29,090
a lot,

16
00:00:29,090 --> 00:00:31,970
and that made me want to visit Denmark.

17
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:36,560
We hardly ever did, though - my mother usually went on her own.

18
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:41,992
But whenever her relations or friends were in England they always came to see

19
00:00:41,992 --> 00:00:42,380
us.

20
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:42,789
BETH:

21
00:00:42,789 --> 00:00:43,606
I see.

22
00:00:43,606 --> 00:00:49,330
So I assume you already speak Danish, one of the languages you'll be studying.

23
00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:49,721
JAMES:

24
00:00:49,721 --> 00:00:55,190
I can get by when I talk to people, though I'm not terribly accurate.

25
00:00:55,190 --> 00:00:55,545
BETH:

26
00:00:55,545 --> 00:01:00,508
Now you probably know that you'll spend the third year of the course abroad.

27
00:01:00,508 --> 00:01:02,990
Have you had any thoughts about that?

28
00:01:02,990 --> 00:01:03,348
JAMES:

29
00:01:03,348 --> 00:01:05,496
I'm really looking forward to it.

30
00:01:05,496 --> 00:01:08,717
And although Denmark seems the obvious place to go,

31
00:01:08,717 --> 00:01:13,370
because of my family connections, I'd love to spend the time in Iceland.

32
00:01:13,370 --> 00:01:13,696
BETH:

33
00:01:13,696 --> 00:01:15,979
Oh, I'm sure it can be arranged.

34
00:01:15,979 --> 00:01:18,913
Do you have any plans for when you graduate?

35
00:01:18,913 --> 00:01:22,500
A lot of students go on to take a master's degree.

36
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:22,869
JAMES:

37
00:01:22,869 --> 00:01:28,041
I think the four years of the undergraduate course will be enough for me.

38
00:01:28,041 --> 00:01:33,212
I'm interested in journalism and I quite like the idea of moving to Scandinavia

39
00:01:33,212 --> 00:01:34,690
and writing for magazines.

40
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:38,131
I'd find that more creative than translating,

41
00:01:38,131 --> 00:01:41,080
which I suppose most graduates do.

42
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:41,498
BETH:

43
00:01:41,498 --> 00:01:41,916
OK.

44
00:01:41,916 --> 00:01:46,930
Now how are you finding the courses you're taking this term, James?

45
00:01:46,930 --> 00:01:47,336
JAMES:

46
00:01:47,336 --> 00:01:50,990
Well, I'm really enjoying the one on Swedish cinema.

47
00:01:50,990 --> 00:01:51,386
BETH:

48
00:01:51,386 --> 00:01:52,968
That'll continue next term,

49
00:01:52,968 --> 00:01:58,507
but the one on Scandinavian literature that's running at the moment will be replaced

50
00:01:58,507 --> 00:02:00,090
by more specialised courses.

51
00:02:00,090 --> 00:02:00,456
Oh,

52
00:02:00,456 --> 00:02:05,214
and by the way, if you're interested in watching Danish television programmes - there's

53
00:02:05,214 --> 00:02:09,240
going to be a course on that the term after next.

54
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:09,700
JAMES:

55
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:11,080
That sounds good.

56
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:11,367
BETH:

57
00:02:11,367 --> 00:02:15,391
Have you started thinking about the literature paper that you have to write in

58
00:02:15,391 --> 00:02:16,540
the next few weeks?

59
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:16,946
JAMES:

60
00:02:16,946 --> 00:02:22,220
Yes, my first choice would be to do something on the Icelandic sagas.

61
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:22,557
BETH:

62
00:02:22,557 --> 00:02:22,894
Hmm.

63
00:02:22,894 --> 00:02:27,277
The trouble with that is that a lot of people choose that topic,

64
00:02:27,277 --> 00:02:31,660
and it can be difficult to get hold of the books you'll need.

65
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:34,410
Why not leave that for another time?

66
00:02:34,410 --> 00:02:34,970
JAMES:

67
00:02:34,970 --> 00:02:35,530
Right.

68
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:35,954
BETH:

69
00:02:35,954 --> 00:02:40,190
You might find modern novels or 19th century playwrights interesting.

70
00:02:40,190 --> 00:02:40,581
JAMES:

71
00:02:40,581 --> 00:02:43,706
I've read or seen several plays in translation,

72
00:02:43,706 --> 00:02:46,440
so that would be a good idea.

73
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:46,854
BETH:

74
00:02:46,854 --> 00:02:47,269
Fine.

75
00:02:47,269 --> 00:02:50,170
I'll put you down for that topic.

76
00:02:50,170 --> 00:02:50,476
JAMES:

77
00:02:50,476 --> 00:02:50,783
Right.

78
00:02:50,783 --> 00:02:54,460
So what would you advise me to aim at in the paper?

79
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:54,791
BETH:

80
00:02:54,791 --> 00:02:59,428
First I suggest you avoid taking one writer and going into a great deal

81
00:02:59,428 --> 00:03:00,090
of detail.

82
00:03:00,090 --> 00:03:02,079
That approach certainly has its place,

83
00:03:02,079 --> 00:03:06,719
but I think you first need to get an understanding of the literature in

84
00:03:06,719 --> 00:03:11,027
the context of the society in which it was produced - who it was

85
00:03:11,027 --> 00:03:13,016
written for, how it was published,

86
00:03:13,016 --> 00:03:14,010
and so on.

87
00:03:14,010 --> 00:03:18,891
I also think that's more fruitful than placing it within the history of the

88
00:03:18,891 --> 00:03:19,240
genre.

89
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:19,804
JAMES:

90
00:03:19,804 --> 00:03:20,368
OK,

91
00:03:20,368 --> 00:03:22,060
that sounds reasonable.

92
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:22,370
JAMES:

93
00:03:22,370 --> 00:03:26,710
Could I ask for some advice about writing the paper I'm working on about

94
00:03:26,710 --> 00:03:27,330
the Vikings?

95
00:03:27,330 --> 00:03:29,500
I have to do that this week,

96
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:31,050
and I'm a bit stuck.

97
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:31,410
BETH:

98
00:03:31,410 --> 00:03:32,130
Of course.

99
00:03:32,130 --> 00:03:35,010
Have you decided yet what to write about?

100
00:03:35,010 --> 00:03:35,562
JAMES:

101
00:03:35,562 --> 00:03:37,219
No, I haven't.

102
00:03:37,219 --> 00:03:44,400
There's so much that seems interesting - Viking settlement in other countries, trade, mythology ...

103
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:44,733
BETH:

104
00:03:44,733 --> 00:03:49,399
Well, what I suggest is that you read an assignment a student wrote last

105
00:03:49,399 --> 00:03:49,732
year,

106
00:03:49,732 --> 00:03:51,731
which is kept in the library.

107
00:03:51,731 --> 00:03:53,397
It's short and well focused,

108
00:03:53,397 --> 00:03:55,730
and I'm sure you'll find it helpful.

109
00:03:55,730 --> 00:03:58,721
I'll give you the details in a moment.

110
00:03:58,721 --> 00:04:03,956
Textbooks usually cover so many topics, it can be very difficult to choose just

111
00:04:03,956 --> 00:04:04,330
one.

112
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:04,678
JAMES:

113
00:04:04,678 --> 00:04:05,026
OK.

114
00:04:05,026 --> 00:04:09,895
I've got a DVD of the film about the Vikings that came out earlier

115
00:04:09,895 --> 00:04:10,591
this year.

116
00:04:10,591 --> 00:04:12,330
Should I watch that again?

117
00:04:12,330 --> 00:04:12,738
BETH:

118
00:04:12,738 --> 00:04:18,047
If it's the one I am thinking of, hmm, I'd ignore it - it's

119
00:04:18,047 --> 00:04:19,680
more fantasy than reality.

120
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:23,764
But I've got a recording of a documentary that you should watch.

121
00:04:23,764 --> 00:04:26,147
It makes some interesting and provocative points,

122
00:04:26,147 --> 00:04:29,550
which I think will help you to focus your topic.

123
00:04:29,550 --> 00:04:31,120
JAMES:Right.

124
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:31,501
JAMES:

125
00:04:31,501 --> 00:04:34,550
So then should I work out an outline?

126
00:04:34,550 --> 00:04:34,916
BETH:

127
00:04:34,916 --> 00:04:35,282
Yes.

128
00:04:35,282 --> 00:04:38,212
Just headings for different sections, at this stage.

129
00:04:38,212 --> 00:04:43,338
And then you should start looking for suitable articles and books to draw on,

130
00:04:43,338 --> 00:04:47,000
and take notes which you organise according to those headings.

131
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:47,527
JAMES:

132
00:04:47,527 --> 00:04:48,580
I see.

133
00:04:48,580 --> 00:04:48,958
BETH:

134
00:04:48,958 --> 00:04:53,490
Then put short phrases and sentences as bullet points under each heading.

135
00:04:53,490 --> 00:04:58,777
Make sure that this skeleton makes sense and flows properly, before writing up the

136
00:04:58,777 --> 00:04:59,910
paper in full.

137
00:04:59,910 --> 00:05:00,665
JAMES:

138
00:05:00,665 --> 00:05:01,420
OK.

139
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:02,175
Thanks,

140
00:05:02,175 --> 00:05:04,440
that's very helpful.
